WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:03.620
Film Courage: How does a writer determine
their strengths and weaknesses?

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:04.990
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: Good
question.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:11.770
A lot of times a writer’s strength could…I
was thinking back to writing partners which

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:18.340
I had at one time for a number of years and
what our strengths were and my partner’s

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.900
strength was coming up with the idea.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.600
He would have a dream and say “We’re having
coffee in the morning.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.050
I have our next movie.”

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.660
That wouldn’t be such a strength of mine
because I am very picky about ideas and they

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.620
come few and far between for me.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.640
Some people are like everything is an idea
for a movie.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.640
It kind of isn’t.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.100
You have to work more at some and others are
definitely not and just boom.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.600
So his strength was coming up with the idea.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:55.079
My strength was for us to be able to sit down
and write the idea you know structure all

00:00:55.079 --> 00:01:01.150
this kind so he knew as well but I was the
one at the keyboard and so sometimes a writer

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.610
can also have a strength of dialogue.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.700
So let’s say a partner is really good with
dialogue and the other is good with structure

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.820
or both of them are good at both.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:17.860
But I think when the more that you write and
find what your strengths and weaknesses are

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:23.700
will be from what notes you receive over time
and time again like well structure-wise this

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.920
is off and your dialogue is great, your character
is meh…you know what I mean?

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:36.380
So you can only find out what your strengths
and weaknesses by doing it and then when you

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:43.290
find a weakness try to build upon that and
get better.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.290
Make it a strength.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:49.540
Film Courage: So it’s only by getting feedback
and if you don’t have a writing partner,

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.210
sending it out for notes, having a friend?

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:58.840
Mark: Yes or writer’s groups or a consultant
or someone to read it and you know these things

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:08.060
take time of course and let’s say your strength
is that you got to page 100 and you may have

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:20.139
all the strengths but everybody has a weakness
that has to be built upon to have it all be

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:21.139
built up at the same level.

00:02:21.139 --> 00:02:27.510
What you want to do is calibrate that and
it will take time but I think from feedback

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:34.340
because you will see what am I not doing right?

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.689
What’s not hitting the mark?

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.739
Well your dialogue isn’t…it all sounds
the same.

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:46.569
I’m kind of weak at dialogue but I can really
craft a great structure that type of thing.

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:52.099
I think after two or three more scripts maybe
you start to say well I should really study

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:55.110
dialogue like we were talking about.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:02.730
Record it and be out in the world, listen
to inflection, tone, diction (things like

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:03.730
that).

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:12.040
Film Courage: I am always shocked how…I
mean personal conversations people have on

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.040
cell phones.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.310
I guess you could get some incredible stories.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:20.209
I am always surprised people are willing to
share such personal information in a public

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:21.209
space.

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:22.209
Mark: Oh sure, and you only hear the one side.

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:26.669
Film Courage: And I’ve heard some doozies
in my time just being in a store and like

00:03:26.669 --> 00:03:27.669
oh my goodness.

00:03:27.669 --> 00:03:29.279
Either this person doesn’t care…

00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:30.839
Mark: The test came back.

00:03:30.839 --> 00:03:34.159
Film Courage: I’ve heard all sorts of stuff.

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:40.170
Mark: And you can only imagine what’s being
said on this other side.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:47.519
Which is a technique in a scene too where
can only know what is on this side and our

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:48.559
mind type of thing.

00:03:48.559 --> 00:03:54.510
Film Courage: That’s true because it keeps…I’m
thinking who is this other person on the other

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:55.510
end?

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.239
And usually it’s someone who is not doing
a lot of talking.

00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:03.739
Mark: Or they don’t say “Hi Honey,”
or whatever it’s “What’s going on here?”

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:08.709
With that you’d say…I love how people
say “That’s a good idea for a movie!”

00:04:08.709 --> 00:04:11.079
You go “It’s not, what is it?”

00:04:11.079 --> 00:04:17.530
Okay it’s a kernel which could be built
but what is it about?

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:21.489
Or someone comes to your party and says “Hey,
you right scripts don’t you?”

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:22.720
“Yeah…when I can.”

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.620
And they go “I gotta good idea for a movie.”

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:25.639
You’re like “Great.

00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:26.639
You should write it!”

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:27.639
And they go “Oh?

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:28.639
I’m not a writer.”

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:31.300
I say “Well you want me to write your idea.”

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:32.620
They say “Yeah, it’s great.”

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.550
And then they pitch it to you and you go “Yeah
okay.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:36.550
I’ll think about it.”

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:41.720
And not to…but sometimes every idea is an
idea for a movie.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.250
If it’s worked on yes.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.440
Sometimes like we were talking about you hammer
it down and walk away, it’s not.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.440
Question For The Viewers: Which part of the
writing process do you love?

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:50.660
Which part do you hate?

